{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"b6a80e3d3d1d599a32ff6a77c68524981eb2b7ba545dde52fa8e42e5262cc5d9","slug":"introduction","location":"/chapters/ch05_semantic_search/5_1_introduction.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Introduction","authors":[{"id":"Roger Weber","name":"Roger Weber"}],"content_includes_title":false,"numbering":{"title":{"offset":2}},"thumbnail":"C:/Program Files/Git/mmir-unibasel-hs26/build/figure_5_2-b7b3b2b1fd5c36c4161a169782ac570f.png","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"5_1_introduction.md","url":"C:/Program Files/Git/mmir-unibasel-hs26/build/5_1_introduction-e3294695d40a8151b9709687465853e2.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"A user searching for “heart disease” expects to find documents about myocardial infarction, coronary artery conditions, and cardiology treatment, even when those documents share no keywords with the query. Classical retrieval models based on BM25 or the vector space model treat terms as independent symbols: they can match “heart” to “heart” but not to “cardiac”. Stemming and lemmatization help at the surface level, and hand-curated ontologies can map synonyms, but these approaches are labor-intensive, language-specific, and brittle when facing the full diversity of natural language expression.","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"bSjsEm8ofD"}],"key":"xUed1LjYyb"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Semantic search solves this problem by representing text as dense vectors in a continuous space where geometric proximity encodes meaning. Two words that never co-occur in a query-document pair can still be recognized as related if their embeddings point in similar directions. This shift from discrete term matching to continuous meaning matching is the central theme of this chapter.","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"uQkJXwXMjV"}],"key":"BEeOS8vJpD"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"C:/Program Files/Git/mmir-unibasel-hs26/build/figure_5_2-b7b3b2b1fd5c36c4161a169782ac570f.png","alt":"Keyword matching operates on exact surface forms (left). Semantic matching maps terms into a continuous embedding space where conceptually related terms cluster together (right), enabling retrieval even when query and document share no words.","width":"90%","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"MiOCERmmeZ","urlSource":"images/figure_5_2.png"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"fig-keyword-vs-semantic","identifier":"fig-keyword-vs-semantic","html_id":"fig-keyword-vs-semantic","enumerator":"1","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"wToUNjKhmh"},{"type":"text","value":"1","key":"sKdHgHGFAY"},{"type":"text","value":":","key":"M57UpDKrS8"}],"template":"Figure %s:","key":"VlzudKHJFQ"},{"type":"text","value":"Keyword matching operates on exact surface forms (left). Semantic matching maps terms into a continuous embedding space where conceptually related terms cluster together (right), enabling retrieval even when query and document share no words.","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"loGirG0C3G"}],"key":"o3rhEOkahF"}],"key":"SIB4vCdFCn"}],"label":"fig-keyword-vs-semantic","identifier":"fig-keyword-vs-semantic","enumerator":"1","html_id":"fig-keyword-vs-semantic","key":"OS6PY1Pbmt"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Evolution of Semantic Representations","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"X7tvQ4mKCS"}],"identifier":"the-evolution-of-semantic-representations","label":"The Evolution of Semantic Representations","html_id":"the-evolution-of-semantic-representations","implicit":true,"key":"UopwlBDFPY"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The path from keyword retrieval to modern semantic search spans three decades of research, each generation addressing limitations of its predecessor:","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"svTXblNyEv"}],"key":"BT3qU7q58O"},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Latent Semantic Indexing","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"VDnsYiKxPq"}],"key":"K4XFmhvUmw"},{"type":"text","value":" (Deerwester et al., 1988) applied Singular Value Decomposition to the term-document matrix, discovering latent topics that link related terms. LSI proved that dimensionality reduction could capture semantic relationships, but its computational cost and inability to use inverted indices limited practical adoption.","position":{"start":{"line":28,"column":1},"end":{"line":28,"column":1}},"key":"JcRoxp5Nbz"}],"key":"QcflxVrGFB"}],"key":"Z8T9nawFnt"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":31,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Static word embeddings","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"psGCT2upvl"}],"key":"ZOPNgRee9r"},{"type":"text","value":" (Mikolov et al., 2013; Pennington et al., 2014) learned dense vector representations from local context windows. Word2Vec and GloVe showed that simple neural architectures trained on large corpora produce vectors where semantic similarity corresponds to geometric proximity. However, each word receives a single vector regardless of context: “bank” has the same representation whether it refers to a financial institution or a riverbank.","position":{"start":{"line":30,"column":1},"end":{"line":30,"column":1}},"key":"sJtnSZbuu0"}],"key":"CULlLobqOi"}],"key":"fwV3jkKubf"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":33,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Contextual embeddings and transformers","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"AVKNi7keyE"}],"key":"zJHJr1l3Qa"},{"type":"text","value":" (Vaswani et al., 2017; Devlin et al., 2019; Reimers and Gurevych, 2019) introduced attention mechanisms that produce different representations for the same word depending on its surrounding context. Sentence-BERT restructured transformers into efficient bi-encoders that generate high-quality sentence embeddings suitable for large-scale retrieval.","position":{"start":{"line":32,"column":1},"end":{"line":32,"column":1}},"key":"s0YBv5EoLM"}],"key":"wuIdUgaGV9"}],"key":"IqJSzbXKax"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":35,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Modern retrieval architectures","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"mUirptpojg"}],"key":"UZ0RTPldL6"},{"type":"text","value":" (Khattab and Zaharia, 2020; Kusupati et al., 2022) introduced late interaction models like ColBERT that balance quality and efficiency, Matryoshka embeddings that enable multi-resolution search, and hybrid pipelines that combine sparse and dense retrieval for robustness.","position":{"start":{"line":34,"column":1},"end":{"line":34,"column":1}},"key":"F2sIdKaOTS"}],"key":"YdGSFE1p8J"}],"key":"rXcaWbyXbb"}],"key":"T8rHqh4z4c"},{"type":"container","kind":"figure","children":[{"type":"image","url":"C:/Program Files/Git/mmir-unibasel-hs26/build/figure_5_1-f65b0bea9057e74313f6bad730830b81.png","alt":"The modern retrieval pipeline: a fast retriever selects candidate documents from the index, then a re-ranker applies deeper semantic analysis to produce the final ranked list.","width":"80%","data":{"altTextIsAutoGenerated":true},"key":"Vgo9ONplYY","urlSource":"images/figure_5_1.png"},{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"figure","label":"fig-retrieval-pipeline","identifier":"fig-retrieval-pipeline","html_id":"fig-retrieval-pipeline","enumerator":"2","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure ","key":"RCs5oWsb7P"},{"type":"text","value":"2","key":"o0SxIvwEL0"},{"type":"text","value":":","key":"Xe6fRZD7hD"}],"template":"Figure %s:","key":"k3w8cbW6wu"},{"type":"text","value":"The modern retrieval pipeline: a fast retriever selects candidate documents from the index, then a re-ranker applies deeper semantic analysis to produce the final ranked list.","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"lin